hi and welcome to my video series of  mall bio explained in three minutes  where i explain a concept of molecular  biology in three minutes or so if you  haven't yet subscribed the channel  hit that subscribe button right now  today we'll talk about inverse pcr which  is a variant of  pcr that is used to amplify your dna  with only one known sequence inverse  pcr is specially useful for  determination of  insert location let me tell you with the  example for a better understanding  let's say we have a transposon and this  transposon  can jump into any genetic loci right  in the genome so we need to understand  that what is the genomic site where this  transposon  has incorporated itself in order to know  that  we can perform a inverse pcr  let's try to understand it in details  so here is our transposon insertion site  what we know from our knowledge is what  is the sequence of the transposon  but what we don't know is the nearby  region  sequence where the transposon has  incorporated itself  but we have something to begin with  unknown sequence which is the transposon  sequence  now what can be done is a little bit bit  sequence flanking these  unknown sequence can be restriction  digested  and then ligated  now this ligated circular plasmid-like  structure  can be pcr amplified using  a dedicated set of primers  so here you can see these pcr primers  can amplify the whole portion of these  circular plasmid like structure and  ultimately  the pcr amplicons would be generated  now in each pcr amplicon we can have  the sequences the known sequence  flanked by unknown sequences on either  side of it  now if we take the pcr product and then  run this pcr product through a  sequencing machine  we would understand the exact sequence  since we know the sequence of our  transposon we would understand what are  the sequences just  side of this particular transposon and  that would tell us  what is the genetic loci so once we get  the sequence information  we can perform bioinformatic analysis  and blast that particular  sequence into a genomic database and  then it would tell us that which  particular genetic loci  our transposon has uh jumped in or get  got incorporated so this is how inverse  pcr helps us to understand  to find unknown sequences with the help  of one known sequence  and that's all about this video if you  like this video  you can support me on patreon  and also in other applications such as  pim upi  if you like this video give it a quick  thumbs up don't forget to like share and  subscribe thank you
 

